{
  "subtype": "what_if",
  "outcome_variable": "hardware_incident_rate",
  "per_column": [
    {
      "name": "is_hardware_incident",
      "causal_role": "outcome",
      "named_in_query": true,
      "rationale": "Flags whether a record is a hardware incident; this operationalizes the outcome (hardware incident rate), not a treatment or confounder."
    },
    {
      "name": "incident_category_group",
      "causal_role": "outcome",
      "named_in_query": true,
      "rationale": "Categorizes incidents by type; largely a finer version of the outcome definition rather than an intervenable lever or confounder."
    },
    {
      "name": "root_cause_hint",
      "causal_role": "treatment",
      "named_in_query": true,
      "rationale": "The query's intervention is 'addressing the most common failure causes'; this column names those causes (e.g. connectivity_config, physical_damage, overheating). However, a root cause is a diagnostic label of what already happened, not itself a settable lever; addressing it is the action, but the column encodes the cause, not an intervention assignment."
    },
    {
      "name": "recurrence_signal",
      "causal_role": "mediator",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Indicates whether an incident recurs; this lies on the pathway between unaddressed causes and future incident counts, so it is a mediator, not a common-cause confounder."
    }
  ],
  "treatment_present": true,
  "treatment_columns": ["root_cause_hint"],
  "treatment_intervenable": false,
  "confounders": [],
  "confounder_quality": 0.0,
  "rationale": "The query poses a clear what-if: intervening on the most common hardware failure causes and reading off the change in hardware incident rate. The added columns capture the outcome (is_hardware_incident, incident_category_group) and enumerate candidate failure causes (root_cause_hint), which corresponds to the treatment concept already named in the query. But root_cause_hint encodes a diagnostic label of what happened, not an actor-settable lever (there is no policy/action/assignment column such as maintenance_schedule, firmware_update_applied, cooling_upgrade, or hardware_replacement that one could actually SET). Recurrence_signal is a mediator, not a confounder. Critically, NO confounders -- common causes of both the intervention and the incident rate -- are provided, and certainly none beyond what the query already names (e.g. asset_age, device_model, environment_temperature, usage_load, vendor). Without adjustable treatment levers and adjustable confounders, the counterfactual effect cannot be identified. The set fails the interventional test."
}

